    "https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/235007": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "NCSU Employment Site | Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Equal Opportunity at NC State News at NC State NC State Named Best Large Employer in North Carolina You Belong Here (Our Employee Value Proposition) Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please provide the follow as part of your application: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Research Statement 4. Teaching Statement Copies of three scholarly works: 5. Other Document 1 6. Other Document 2 7. Other Document 3 Posting Details Posting Information Posting Number PG194841EP Internal Recruitment No Working Title Assistant Professor Anticipated Hiring Range Commensurate with education and credentials ($85,000 - $95,000) Work Schedule Core Business Hours: Monday - Friday; 8:00 am - 5:00 pm; schedule will vary based on research and teaching needs. Job Location Raleigh, NC Department Psychology About the Department NC State is a research extensive land grant institution serving more than 34,000 students and is located in Raleigh, NC, the state capital (http://www.ncsu.edu/about-nc-state/). This geographical location offers access to major intellectual and cultural resources, including the Research Triangle Park, Duke University, University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, and the National Humanities Center. The School Psychology is an AP A-accredited and NASP -approved Ph.D. program. Additional information about the university (http://www.ncsu.edu/) or the department (https://psychology.chass.ncsu.edu/) may be accessed on the web. The Department of Psychology consists of 31 faculty members, the staff of the advising center, the staff of the Psychoeducational Clinic, and the four members of our office staff, as well as over 750 undergraduate majors and 120 graduate students pursuing their doctoral degrees. The faculty are outstanding scholars, many of whom have won awards for teaching and research and/or are fellows in the major professional societies in Psychology. Faculty have received research grants and training grants from the National Science Foundation, the National Institutes for Health, the National Institute on Aging, the Army Research Laboratory, the Army Research Institute, the Department of Education, and numerous companies. Wolfpack Perks and Benefits As a Pack member, you belong here, and can enjoy exclusive perks designed to enhance your personal and professional well-being. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our Employee Value Proposition and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone. What we offer: Medical , Dental , and Vision Flexible Spending Account Retirement Programs Disability Plans Life Insurance Accident Plan Paid Time Off and Other Leave Programs 12 Holidays Each Year Tuition and Academic Assistance And so much more! Attain Work-life balance with our Childcare benefits, Wellness & Recreation Membership , and Wellness Programs that aim to build a thriving wolfpack community. Disclaimer: Perks and Benefit eligibility is based on Part-Time or Full-Time Employment status. Eligibility and Employer Sponsored Plans can be found within each of the links offered. Essential Job Duties The Department of Psychology at NC State University is seeking a tenure-track Assistant Professor to advance research, teaching, and service in Lifespan Developmental Psychology beginning Fall 2027. We invite scholars who conduct applied developmental research at any stage of the lifespan, including infancy, childhood, adolescence, adulthood, and/or aging. While the research area is open, we are especially interested in candidates who bridge the gap between laboratory science and real-world impacts that improve lives in varied environments using multimodal data. Faculty members in Lifespan Developmental Psychology conduct both basic and applied research spanning the lifespan, with an emphasis on cognitive, socioemotional, and health-related development. The purpose of this position is to complement and extend these existing strengths while contributing to our collaborative, interdisciplinary approach to understanding development across the lifespan. In this role, you will develop a productive research program that advances understanding of development across the lifespan and contributes meaningful scholarship to the discipline. You will teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, helping them develop as scholars and professionals, while contributing your expertise to the continued growth of the Lifespan Developmental Psychology program and the broader discipline. Key Responsibilities: Develop and sustain an independent research program in lifespan developmental psychology. Disseminate research through scholarly publications, presentations, and other professional outlets. Teach courses that support undergraduate and graduate education in psychology. Mentor undergraduate and graduate students in their academics, research, and professional development. Contribute service and professional leadership within the Lifespan Developmental Psychology program area, department, college, university, and broader field. If you are a scholar in lifespan developmental psychology who is excited to build a meaningful research program, connect laboratory science with real-world impact, engage students through teaching and mentorship, and contribute to a collaborative academic community, we encourage you to apply. Other Responsibilities In addition to the core responsibilities of this role, there will be occasional opportunities to take on additional duties. This offers an opportunity to build on your skills, explore new areas, and gain a deeper understanding of the department’s operations. Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience Requires a doctoral degree in Psychology with a Developmental Psychology focus or other related fields. Other Required Qualifications A record of scholarly research appropriate to the career stage, demonstrated through publications, presentations, or other scholarly outlets. Capacity to teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels and mentor students in research and professional development. Knowledge of developmental processes across one or more stages of the lifespan, with the ability to contribute to courses across various domains and phases of development. Capacity to work effectively in interdisciplinary collaborative teams within and beyond the university. Preferred Qualifications Research expertise that complements or extends the department’s strengths in cognitive, socioemotional, and/or health-related development. Knowledge of or experience with multimodal approaches to developmental research. A research orientation that connects laboratory science with real-world applications and meaningful impacts on individuals and communities. Scholarly interests that contribute to the growth and development of the Lifespan Developmental Psychology program. Required License(s) or Certification(s) n/a Valid NC Driver's License required No Commercial Driver's License required No Recruitment Dates and Special Instructions Job Open Date 09/14/2026 Anticipated Close Date 10/26/2026; If posting is still active we are accepting and reviewing applications Special Instructions to Applicants Please provide the follow as part of your application: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Research Statement 4. Teaching Statement Copies of three scholarly works: 5. Other Document 1 6. Other Document 2 7. Other Document 3 Position Details Position Number 00000461 Position Type Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Full Time Equivalent (FTE) (1.0 = 40 hours/week) 1.00 Appointment 9 Month Recurring Mandatory Designation - Adverse Weather Non Mandatory - Adverse Weather Mandatory Designation - Emergency Events Non Mandatory - Emergency Event Department ID 164501 - Psychology EEO NC State University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive equal opportunities for employment without regard to age, color, disability, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, and veteran status. The University encourages all qualified applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities, to apply. Individuals with disabilities requiring disability-related accommodations in the application and interview process are welcome to contact 919-513-0574 to speak with a representative of the Office of Equal Opportunity. If you have general questions about the application process, you may contact Human Resources at (919) 515-2135 or workatncstate@ncsu.edu . Final candidates are subject to criminal & sex offender background checks. Some vacancies also require credit or motor vehicle checks. Degree(s) must be obtained prior to start date in order to meet qualifications and receive credit. NC State University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this opportunity? NCSU Website Job Alert (automated email from our system) NCSU Executive Search Services Monster.com CareerBuilder.com InsideHigherEd.com Other Online Job Board Carolina Job Finder / Employment Guide Job / Career Fair The Chronicle of Higher Education Professional Journal Print Advertisement (Newspaper / Periodical) Professional Organization Direct Contact from NCSU HR Representative / Recruiter NCSU Employee Referral Social Media (LinkedIn, Twitter, Facebook, Other) Other If you learned about this vacancy from \"other source\" or \"other website\", please provide the source. (Open Ended Question) Application Materials Required Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Other Document (1) Other Document (2) Other Document (3) Contact Information for References Research Statement Teaching Statement Optional Documents NC STATE UNIVERSITY RALEIGH, NC 27695 PHONE: (919) 515-2011 Privacy Accessibility Emergency Information Policies NC State Home © 2019 NC State University. All rights reserved To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/234932": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Elementary Teacher Education",
      "description": "NCSU Employment Site | Assistant Professor in Elementary Teacher Education Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Equal Opportunity at NC State News at NC State NC State Named Best Large Employer in North Carolina You Belong Here (Our Employee Value Proposition) Assistant Professor in Elementary Teacher Education Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please include the following as a part of your application packet: 1. Cover Letter 2. CV 3. Teaching Statement 4. Contact information for References Posting Details Posting Information Posting Number PG194860EP Internal Recruitment No Working Title Assistant Professor in Elementary Teacher Education Anticipated Hiring Range $80,000 - $84,000 Work Schedule 40 hours a week Job Location Raleigh NC Department TELS About the Department About the NC State College of Education As one of the nation’s top-ranked colleges of education, NC State’s College of Education leads the way in North Carolina in preparing extraordinary educators, conducting transformational research, and engaging communities where they are to improve the educational outcomes of all learners and advance the greater good. The college’s faculty are widely-recognized thought leaders who use their research to transform the practice of teaching, learning, and leading. They have particular strengths in improving technology-enhanced learning and teaching; fostering community-based learning and PK-16 partnerships; and innovating leadership and workforce development, educational policy, and evaluation. About two-thirds of the faculty are grant active. They are engaged in over 225 active projects totaling $151 million, making them the most productive in terms of research activity compared with other colleges of education in North Carolina and among the Top 5% in the nation. In FY24, the college’s research expenditures exceeded $20 million. The college has over 1,600 students studying across five graduate certificates, seven undergraduate majors, two undergraduate minors, 13 master’s degrees, three Ph.D., and two Ed.D. programs housed in three academic departments: Educational Leadership, Policy, and Human Development; Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics Education; and Teacher Education and Learning Sciences, with approximately 130 tenure-track and professional-track faculty, 110 support and professional staff, and about 160 graduate and teaching assistants. The College of Education is also home to the Belk Center for Community College Leadership and Research, the Friday Institute for Educational Innovation, and Elevate, NC State’s inclusive postsecondary education program for students with intellectual and developmental disabilities. The college has over 18,000 living alumni, with over 6,000 working in public schools in every county in North Carolina. Those who are teachers rate among the highest in effectiveness on surveys of school employers in North Carolina. NC State promotes an integrated approach to problem solving that transforms lives and provides leadership for social, economic, and technological development across North Carolina and around the world. NC State’s land grant mission of teaching, research and service is dedicated to the service of North Carolina and its people. Applicants are encouraged to review the institution’s mission, vision and strategic plan, and consider how their background, interest and experience would enable them to support the university. The Department of Teacher Education and Learning Sciences ( TELS ) comprises over 40 full-time faculty members preparing more than 500 undergraduate and graduate students and 200 students in alternative teacher licensure programs. We prepare students to work in a range of fields across various programs that include Elementary Education (with a STEM focus), Educational Psychology, Educational Equity, Literacy and English Language Arts, Learning Design and Technology, Middle and Secondary English Language Arts, Social Studies, Multilingual Education, and Special Education. We advance education through scholarship, leadership, and advocacy. We prepare professionals who are committed to provide learning opportunities for all and who have deep content knowledge, demonstrate strong working knowledge of effective pedagogies, and realize the potential of digital technologies to enhance learning. We shape the field of education by engaging in research that addresses current challenges, and by participating in cross-disciplinary work that deals with real problems in forward-looking ways. The department exemplifies an innovative merging of teacher education and the learning sciences that results in new pathways for teaching, scholarship, leadership, and service. The preparation that our students receive is unmatched in scope, priorities, and outcomes, assuring that our graduates are able to make profound contributions to education and society. In addition to the degree earning programs above, TELS also offers an inclusive postsecondary education program, Elevate. Elevate is a university-wide initiative designed for North Carolina residents with intellectual and developmental disabilities ( IDD ) and offers educational credentials, such as certificates. Wolfpack Perks and Benefits As a Pack member, you belong here, and can enjoy exclusive perks designed to enhance your personal and professional well-being. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our Employee Value Proposition and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone. What we offer: Medical , Dental , and Vision Flexible Spending Account Retirement Programs Disability Plans Life Insurance Accident Plan Paid Time Off and Other Leave Programs 12 Holidays Each Year Tuition and Academic Assistance And so much more! Attain Work-life balance with our Childcare benefits, Wellness & Recreation Membership , and Wellness Programs that aim to build a thriving wolfpack community. Disclaimer: Perks and Benefit eligibility is based on Part-Time or Full-Time Employment status. Eligibility and Employer Sponsored Plans can be found within each of the links offered. Essential Job Duties The Department of Teacher Education and Learning Sciences ( TELS ) invites applications for an Assistant Professor in Elementary Teacher Education to be affiliated with undergraduate and graduate studies in elementary teaching and learning. This is a tenure-track appointment at the Assistant Professor level, with teaching, scholarship, service, and outreach expectations. We are seeking those who are scholars in elementary teacher education who will lead and contribute to research on approaches and programs that prepare pre-service teachers. You will engage in research primarily focused on elementary teacher preparation programs’ features and impact. This might include elements such as modality (e.g., face-to-face, hybrid, online), structure (e.g., traditional four-year programs, part-time programs for practicing teachers, competency-based education), or pathway (e.g., bachelor’s degree, master’s degree, residency, registered apprenticeship). Additionally, you will submit proposals for internal/external funding in support of the related research. The ideal candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in elementary teacher preparation, including the Master of Arts in Teaching ( MAT ) program, with a strong preference for those who teach elementary social studies education. As a faculty member, you will provide service and leadership to the Elementary Education program area, the department, college, and university. You will participate in outreach and engagement activities with elementary schools locally and across North Carolina, and collaborate with faculty within the department, college, and across the university. Position Responsibilities Maintain a high level of scholarly research, publication, and grant-writing activity that aligns with a tenure track position in a university with high research activity. Produce high quality research on pre-service teacher preparation programs, features, and impact. Seek external funding for research and development activities. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in elementary education, with a strong preference for social studies pre-service teacher education graduate and undergraduate programs Recruit, advise, and mentor graduate students and serve on doctoral committees. Engage in collaborative research with other faculty and researchers within the College of Education, including the Friday Institute for Educational Innovation and the Belk Center for Community College leadership, as well as across the university. Provide service and leadership in the elementary education program, department, college, university, and professional organizations. Participate in outreach and engagement for department and college initiatives with underserved communities and high need areas and engaging with stakeholders across the state of North Carolina. If you are passionate about preparing future elementary educators and interested in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in elementary teacher preparation, including the Master of Arts in Teaching ( MAT ) program, with expertise in elementary social studies education, we encourage you to apply and join our faculty. Other Responsibilities N/A Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience Doctorate in Teacher Education, Curriculum & Instruction, Elementary Education, or a related field Other Required Qualifications Experience teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses in elementary education Experience in pre-service teacher preparation K-6 teaching experience Scholarship and funding record commensurate with experience Demonstrated potential for excellence in research, teaching, extension, and service Demonstrated ability to work effectively with students and individuals from a wide range of backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences Demonstrated ability to support students with varied academic and professional needs Preferred Qualifications At least 3 years of K-6 teaching experience A record of publication and presentation commensurate with experience, with preference to work related to the scholarship of teaching and learning Experience facilitating teacher professional development Success in building relationships with schools and districts Required License(s) or Certification(s) N.A Valid NC Driver's License required No Commercial Driver's License required No Recruitment Dates and Special Instructions Job Open Date 09/03/2026 Anticipated Close Date 10/16/2026 Special Instructions to Applicants Please include the following as a part of your application packet: 1. Cover Letter 2. CV 3. Teaching Statement 4. Contact information for References Position Details Position Number 00112293 Position Type Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Full Time Equivalent (FTE) (1.0 = 40 hours/week) 1.0 Appointment 9 Month Recurring Mandatory Designation - Adverse Weather Non Mandatory - Adverse Weather Mandatory Designation - Emergency Events Non Mandatory - Emergency Event Department ID 131101 - Teacher Education and Learning Sciences (TELS) EEO NC State University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive equal opportunities for employment without regard to age, color, disability, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, and veteran status. The University encourages all qualified applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities, to apply. Individuals with disabilities requiring disability-related accommodations in the application and interview process are welcome to contact 919-513-0574 to speak with a representative of the Office of Equal Opportunity. If you have general questions about the application process, you may contact Human Resources at (919) 515-2135 or workatncstate@ncsu.edu . Final candidates are subject to criminal & sex offender background checks. Some vacancies also require credit or motor vehicle checks. Degree(s) must be obtained prior to start date in order to meet qualifications and receive credit. NC State University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this opportunity? NCSU Website Job Alert (automated email from our system) NCSU Executive Search Services Monster.com CareerBuilder.com InsideHigherEd.com Other Online Job Board Carolina Job Finder / Employment Guide Job / Career Fair The Chronicle of Higher Education Professional Journal Print Advertisement (Newspaper / Periodical) Professional Organization Direct Contact from NCSU HR Representative / Recruiter NCSU Employee Referral Social Media (LinkedIn, Twitter, Facebook, Other) Other If you learned about this vacancy from \"other source\" or \"other website\", please provide the source. (Open Ended Question) Application Materials Required Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Contact Information for References Teaching Statement Optional Documents NC STATE UNIVERSITY RALEIGH, NC 27695 PHONE: (919) 515-2011 Privacy Accessibility Emergency Information Policies NC State Home © 2019 NC State University. All rights reserved To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
