    "https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/236499": {
      "title": "Open Rank Assistant Teaching Professor",
      "description": "NCSU Employment Site | Open Rank Assistant Teaching Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Equal Opportunity at NC State News at NC State NC State Named Best Large Employer in North Carolina You Belong Here (Our Employee Value Proposition) Open Rank Assistant Teaching Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please submit all required application documents (see Applicant Documents) online. Teaching Evaluations (e.g., summary of student evaluations from recent courses) should be uploaded in the \"Other Document\" upload. Review of applications will begin November 30, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Interviews may begin at any time. Finalists will be asked to give a teaching presentation or demonstration as part of the on-campus interview. Employment is contingent upon verification of the degree and satisfactory completion of a background check. Posting Details Posting Information Posting Number PG194981EP Internal Recruitment No Working Title Open Rank Assistant Teaching Professor Anticipated Hiring Range $75,000-$80,000 Work Schedule 40 hours a week Job Location Raleigh, NC Department Physics and Astronomy About the Department The Physics and Astronomy Department provides: (1) High quality Physics instruction to the University community through courses in introductory physics; (2) educational opportunities for undergraduate and graduate students in physics through high quality curricula, faculty, and research facilities; (3) outreach programs that advance scientific knowledge. Our research programs cover a diverse spectrum including astrophysics, general relativity, condensed matter theory and experiment, computational physics, biophysics, nanoscale science, physics education, experimental nuclear physics, particle and nuclear theory, experimental optics, and experimental atomic and molecular physics. The department enrolls approximately 120 graduate students and 140 undergrad physics majors. The department size is approximately 57 faculty members. While working in this department, you will be expected to foster a supportive and welcoming environment. NC State promotes an integrated approach to problem solving that transforms lives and provides leadership for social, economic, and technological development across North Carolina and around the world. NC State’s land grant mission of teaching, research and service is dedicated to the service of North Carolina and its people. Applicants are encouraged to review the institution’s mission, vision and strategic plan, and consider how their background, interest and experience would enable them to support the university. Wolfpack Perks and Benefits As a Pack member, you belong here, and can enjoy exclusive perks designed to enhance your personal and professional well-being. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our Employee Value Proposition and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone. What we offer: Medical , Dental , and Vision Flexible Spending Account Retirement Programs Disability Plans Life Insurance Accident Plan Paid Time Off and Other Leave Programs 12 Holidays Each Year Tuition and Academic Assistance And so much more! Attain Work-life balance with our Childcare benefits, Wellness & Recreation Membership , and Wellness Programs that aim to build a thriving wolfpack community. Disclaimer: Perks and Benefit eligibility is based on Part-Time or Full-Time Employment status. Eligibility and Employer Sponsored Plans can be found within each of the links offered. Essential Job Duties The successful candidate will devote most of their time to teaching undergraduate astronomy and astrophysics courses offered by the Department of Physics & Astronomy, including introductory courses for the broader university community and courses for physics majors and the astrophysics minor. Duties include preparing and delivering courses, developing and assessing learning outcomes, advising and mentoring students, and participating in department curriculum development, including helping to shape the astrophysics minor. The candidate will provide high-quality, effective instruction in astronomy and astrophysics for the department and the university and help develop and lead the department’s astrophysics minor. Other responsibilities that may occupy a smaller share of time include contributing to the design, review, and ongoing improvement of the astrophysics minor and related courses; academic advising and mentoring of students interested in astronomy and astrophysics; outreach and public engagement in astronomy; serving on department, college, and university committees; and collaborating with other physics and astronomy faculty, the College of Sciences, and other units that serve students taking astronomy courses. Other Responsibilities Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience Strong knowledge of astronomy and astrophysics at the undergraduate level. Ability to design, deliver, and assess engaging courses in lecture, laboratory, and online or hybrid formats, using learning management systems and instructional technology. Excellent written and oral communication skills for diverse audiences. Ability to mentor and advise students and to foster a supportive, welcoming, and inclusive learning environment. Ability to work collaboratively with colleagues on curriculum development and department initiatives. Other Required Qualifications PhD in physics, astrophysics, astronomy, or a related field. College-level teaching experience. Evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as teaching evaluations and a statement of teaching philosophy. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching astronomy and/or astrophysics courses at the undergraduate level. Experience using evidence-based, student-centered teaching methods and assessing student learning. Experience developing or revising courses or curricula, such as a minor, certificate, or program. Experience teaching a range of student audiences, from non-science majors in introductory courses to science and physics majors in upper-level courses. Experience with astronomical observation, data analysis, or computational tools in a teaching setting. Required License(s) or Certification(s) N/A Valid NC Driver's License required No Commercial Driver's License required No Recruitment Dates and Special Instructions Job Open Date 10/21/2026 Anticipated Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Please submit all required application documents (see Applicant Documents) online. Teaching Evaluations (e.g., summary of student evaluations from recent courses) should be uploaded in the “Other Document” upload. Review of applications will begin November 30, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Interviews may begin at any time. Finalists will be asked to give a teaching presentation or demonstration as part of the on-campus interview. Employment is contingent upon verification of the degree and satisfactory completion of a background check. Position Details Position Number 00112403 Position Type Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Full Time Equivalent (FTE) (1.0 = 40 hours/week) 1.0 Appointment 9 Month Recurring Mandatory Designation - Adverse Weather Non Mandatory - Adverse Weather Mandatory Designation - Emergency Events Non Mandatory - Emergency Event Department ID 170601 - Physics and Astronomy EEO NC State University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive equal opportunities for employment without regard to age, color, disability, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, and veteran status. The University encourages all qualified applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities, to apply. Individuals with disabilities requiring disability-related accommodations in the application and interview process are welcome to contact 919-513-0574 to speak with a representative of the Office of Equal Opportunity. If you have general questions about the application process, you may contact Human Resources at (919) 515-2135 or workatncstate@ncsu.edu . Final candidates are subject to criminal & sex offender background checks. Some vacancies also require credit or motor vehicle checks. Degree(s) must be obtained prior to start date in order to meet qualifications and receive credit. NC State University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this opportunity? NCSU Website NCSU Executive Search Services Monster.com CareerBuilder.com InsideHigherEd.com Other Online Job Board Carolina Job Finder / Employment Guide Job / Career Fair The Chronicle of Higher Education HERC Professional Journal Print Advertisement (Newspaper / Periodical) Professional Organization Direct Contact from NCSU HR Representative / Recruiter NCSU Employee Referral Social Media (LinkedIn, Twitter, Facebook, Other) Other If you learned about this vacancy from \"other source\" or \"other website\", please provide the source. (Open Ended Question) Application Materials Required Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Other Document (1) Contact Information for References Brief Statement of Teaching Philosophy and Research Interests Optional Documents NC STATE UNIVERSITY RALEIGH, NC 27695 PHONE: (919) 515-2011 Privacy Accessibility Emergency Information Policies NC State Home © 2019 NC State University. All rights reserved To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.ncsu.edu/postings/236498": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Rare Earths and Critical Minerals",
      "description": "NCSU Employment Site | Assistant Professor - Rare Earths and Critical Minerals Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Equal Opportunity at NC State News at NC State NC State Named Best Large Employer in North Carolina You Belong Here (Our Employee Value Proposition) Assistant Professor - Rare Earths and Critical Minerals Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin immediately. Applicants should upload the following documents as part of their application: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Contact Information for References Research Statement (in addition to a detailed research plan as an executive summary outlining the need for any specialized equipment or infrastructure that your research program will need to be successful at NC State) Teaching Statement highlighting pedagogical approaches, student engagement, and mentoring Other Document (1) - In approximately 500 words, describe how your research aligns with NC State’s Wolfpack 2030 priorities, particularly in interdisciplinary research, innovation, partnerships, societal impact, and national visibility. Include examples of how your research could complement or connect with existing research groups, initiatives, or areas of strength at NC State. Please have three professional references send letters of recommendation to chemadministration@ncsu.edu and list “00112400” in the subject line. Applications must be submitted electronically via the NCSU employment portal. Posting Details Posting Information Posting Number PG194980EP Internal Recruitment No Working Title Assistant Professor - Rare Earths and Critical Minerals Anticipated Hiring Range Commensurate with experience. Work Schedule Varies based on teaching/research schedule. Job Location Raleigh, NC Department Department of Chemistry About the Department The Department of Chemistry at NC State University provides professional education for chemists at the undergraduate and graduate levels; teaches introductory chemistry courses, which are needed by students enrolled in other department programs; advances the discipline of chemistry by conducting research; serves the public by sharing information about chemistry through a variety of mechanisms and services; and commits to creating environments that are in alignment with our mission, vision and values. Our department is home to award-winning faculty and is one of NC State’s top producers of patent and invention disclosures. More information about the department can be found at https://chemistry.sciences.ncsu.edu/ . While working in this department, you will be expected to foster a supportive and welcoming environment. NC State promotes an integrated approach to problem solving that transforms lives and provides leadership for social, economic, and technological development across North Carolina and around the world. NC State’s land grant mission of teaching, research and service is dedicated to the service of North Carolina and its people. Applicants are encouraged to review the institution’s mission, vision and strategic plan, and consider how their background, interest and experience would enable them to support the university. Wolfpack Perks and Benefits As a Pack member, you belong here, and can enjoy exclusive perks designed to enhance your personal and professional well-being. As you consider this opportunity, we encourage you to review our Employee Value Proposition and learn more about what makes NC State the best place to learn and work for everyone. What we offer: Medical , Dental , and Vision Flexible Spending Account Retirement Programs Disability Plans Life Insurance Accident Plan Paid Time Off and Other Leave Programs 12 Holidays Each Year Tuition and Academic Assistance And so much more! Attain Work-life balance with our Childcare benefits, Wellness & Recreation Membership , and Wellness Programs that aim to build a thriving wolfpack community. Disclaimer: Perks and Benefit eligibility is based on Part-Time or Full-Time Employment status. Eligibility and Employer Sponsored Plans can be found within each of the links offered. Essential Job Duties The Department of Chemistry at North Carolina State University invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the broadly-defined area of Chemistry for Rare Earths and Critical Minerals. We seek candidates addressing the fundamental science of rare-earth elements, critical minerals, and strategically important materials across their life cycle – from separations, recovery, and molecular recognition to characterization, transformation, materials synthesis, recycling and reuse. The theme is intentionally broad to recruit an outstanding chemist from any major chemistry subdiscipline: analytical chemistry (for example, topics include elemental speciation, identification, sensing, and process monitoring); organic chemistry (selective ligands, receptors, extractants, membranes, and functional materials); physical chemistry/spectroscopy (electronic structure, photophysics, magnetism, interfaces, and mechanisms); inorganic chemistry (coordination, extraction, and critical-element compounds); and materials chemistry (magnets, catalysts, phosphors, semiconductors, and energy materials). Key Responsibilities: Lead an independent research program and engage in collaborative research at North Carolina State University and the Research Triangle. Develop a robust, externally funded research program. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses at NC State, demonstrating a commitment to excellence in education and mentorship. Mentor graduate students and other trainees and scholars. Other Responsibilities Other duties as assigned. Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience Candidates must possess a PhD in Chemistry or a closely related discipline to be considered for this position. All degrees must be received from appropriately accredited institutions. Other Required Qualifications N/A Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated record of research accomplishments, evidenced by publications and presentations at leading conferences. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, including contributions to scientific teams. Commitment to excellence in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. Commitment to excellence in mentorship of graduate students and other scientists. At least one year of postdoctoral experience is strongly desired. Required License(s) or Certification(s) N/A Valid NC Driver's License required No Commercial Driver's License required No Recruitment Dates and Special Instructions Job Open Date 10/21/2026 Anticipated Close Date 11/17/2026 [If the posting is still active after this date, applications are still being accepted and will be reviewed.] Special Instructions to Applicants Review of applications will begin immediately. Applicants should upload the following documents as part of their application: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Contact Information for References Research Statement (in addition to a detailed research plan as an executive summary outlining the need for any specialized equipment or infrastructure that your research program will need to be successful at NC State) Teaching Statement highlighting pedagogical approaches, student engagement, and mentoring Other Document (1) - In approximately 500 words, describe how your research aligns with NC State’s Wolfpack 2030 priorities, particularly in interdisciplinary research, innovation, partnerships, societal impact, and national visibility. Include examples of how your research could complement or connect with existing research groups, initiatives, or areas of strength at NC State. Please have three professional references send letters of recommendation to chemadministration@ncsu.edu and list “00112400” in the subject line. Applications must be submitted electronically via the NCSU employment portal. Position Details Position Number 00112400 Position Type Tenured/Tenure-Track Faculty Full Time Equivalent (FTE) (1.0 = 40 hours/week) 1.0 Appointment 9 Month Recurring Mandatory Designation - Adverse Weather Non Mandatory - Adverse Weather Mandatory Designation - Emergency Events Non Mandatory - Emergency Event Department ID 170701 - Chemistry EEO NC State University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive equal opportunities for employment without regard to age, color, disability, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, and veteran status. The University encourages all qualified applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities, to apply. Individuals with disabilities requiring disability-related accommodations in the application and interview process are welcome to contact 919-513-0574 to speak with a representative of the Office of Equal Opportunity. If you have general questions about the application process, you may contact Human Resources at (919) 515-2135 or workatncstate@ncsu.edu . Final candidates are subject to criminal & sex offender background checks. Some vacancies also require credit or motor vehicle checks. Degree(s) must be obtained prior to start date in order to meet qualifications and receive credit. NC State University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this opportunity? NCSU Website NCSU Executive Search Services Monster.com CareerBuilder.com InsideHigherEd.com Other Online Job Board Carolina Job Finder / Employment Guide Job / Career Fair The Chronicle of Higher Education HERC Professional Journal Print Advertisement (Newspaper / Periodical) Professional Organization Direct Contact from NCSU HR Representative / Recruiter NCSU Employee Referral Social Media (LinkedIn, Twitter, Facebook, Other) Other If you learned about this vacancy from \"other source\" or \"other website\", please provide the source. (Open Ended Question) Application Materials Required Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Other Document (1) Contact Information for References Research Statement Teaching Statement Optional Documents NC STATE UNIVERSITY RALEIGH, NC 27695 PHONE: (919) 515-2011 Privacy Accessibility Emergency Information Policies NC State Home © 2019 NC State University. All rights reserved To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
